.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-EXPORT 1 "2026-09-27" "ShardPost 0.26.2" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-export \- export an account to an archive, verify it and restore it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl export
.BI \-o " file"
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl archive verify
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I file
.br
.B shardpostctl restore
.RB [ \-account
.IR target ]
.RB [ \-acl ]
.RB [ \-activate-sieve ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
An account can be written to a single archive file and put back from it,
with the IMAP identities intact: mail clients do not have to download
everything again after a restore.
.SS The archive
Format
.IR shardpost-account-export ,
version 1: a tar stream with a header, then per mailbox its description
(id, name, UIDVALIDITY, UIDNEXT, HIGHESTMODSEQ, subscription,
special-use, ACL, annotations), per message a description (UID, GUID,
flags, MODSEQ, internal and save date, POP3 UIDL, size, SHA-256)
immediately followed by the message as stored, then the Sieve scripts,
and last a manifest with the checksum of every entry.
.PP
Not in the archive: subscriptions to mailboxes that do not exist, server
annotations (among them the JMAP identities and the JMAP vacation
response), the script
.B jmap-vacation
(a copy the server writes of that vacation response; a restore skips it
in an older archive too), the trace switch, and the quota limit (it
comes from the user database). The full-text index is not stored either; it is rebuilt
from the messages.
.SS export
Streams the account into
.IR file ,
which must not exist yet, and verifies it afterwards. Role:
.BR export .
.PP
The export does not lock the account. Each mailbox is read as it was when
the export reached it; messages deleted in the meantime are listed in the
manifest as
.IR skipped ,
deleted mailboxes as
.IR skipped_mailboxes .
For an exact point in time, hold the account in the director first.
.PP
If the export fails midway, the archive ends with
.I "complete: false"
and is refused by both
.B archive verify
and
.BR restore .
A download that breaks off leaves no file behind. There is no overall
timeout on the download;
.B \-timeout
only bounds the wait for the response and each pause without data.
.SS archive verify
Reads the archive and checks every message against its description and
every entry against the manifest. It needs no server, so a backup can be
verified where it is kept. Prints what the archive holds.
.SS restore
Verifies the archive completely, then imports it through the account
import: mailboxes with their UIDVALIDITY and ids, messages with UIDs,
flags, MODSEQ, dates and POP3 UIDLs, then UIDNEXT and HIGHESTMODSEQ per
mailbox, the ACLs, the annotations and finally the Sieve scripts. Every
mailbox is checked against the target's inventory and reported as
.IR verified .
Role:
.BR import .
.PP
A restore is repeatable: messages that are already there are skipped, so
a run that broke off can simply be repeated. The target must be empty or
hold the same data; a mailbox with the same name but a different
UIDVALIDITY and messages in it is a conflict.
.PP
While the restore runs, the account is locked like during an import.
Afterwards the change log starts behind the restored state, so clients
with a QRESYNC cache synchronize once in full.
.SS Restoring into another account
With
.B \-account
the archive goes into a different account, which is how you prove a
backup without touching the original. Such a restore is deliberately
isolated: ACLs are
.I not
restored (they would give real accounts rights on the copy) and no Sieve
script is activated (the copy must not send vacation replies or forward
mail).
.B \-acl
and
.B \-activate-sieve
switch that on if you really want it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Where to write the archive. The file must not exist.
.TP
.BI \-account " target"
Restore into this account instead of the exported one.
.TP
.B \-acl
Restore ACLs even into another account.
.TP
.B \-activate-sieve
Activate the exported Sieve script even in another account.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
Back up an account and check the result:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl export -o user.tar user@example.org
shardpostctl archive verify user.tar
.RE
.fi
.PP
Prove the backup without touching the live account:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl restore -account restore-test@example.org user.tar
shardpostctl mailbox list -all restore-test@example.org
.RE
.fi
.PP
Rebuild an account from its archive (this is also how a mailbox created
with an old index shard size gets the current one). Hold the account
first so nothing is delivered in between:
.PP
.nf
.RS
nauthilus-directorctl users hold set user@example.org --duration 15m \\
    --reason mailbox-rebuild
nauthilus-directorctl users kick user@example.org --reason mailbox-rebuild

shardpostctl export -o user.tar user@example.org
shardpostctl plan purge user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
shardpostctl restore user.tar

nauthilus-directorctl users hold clear user@example.org \\
    --reason mailbox-rebuild-complete
.RE
.fi
.PP
Check an old backup on a workstation, without a server:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl archive verify /backup/2026-09-20/user.tar
.RE
.fi
.PP
Inside the cluster the archive is written in the
.I import
sidecar, where a volume with room for it is mounted:
.PP
.nf
.RS
kubectl -n shardpost exec deploy/shardpost-server -c import -- \\
    shardpostctl -timeout 30m export -o /work/user.tar user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done; the archive is intact.
.TP
.B 1
The restore partly failed. Run it again; what is already there is
skipped.
.TP
.B 65
The archive is damaged, incomplete or not a ShardPost archive. Nothing
was restored.
.TP
.B 67
No such account.
.TP
.B 69
The export failed. No file was left behind.
.TP
.B 73
The target file already exists or could not be created.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role
.RB ( export " for the export, " import " for the restore)."
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-migrate (1)
.PP
The archive format and the restore are described in
.IR docs/admin-api.md .
